// Practice quiz: HashMap basics
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, so generic types are written as Map of String to Integer.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/arrays-lists-and-maps/hashmap-basics

::o1 same key twice::A Map of String to String named capitals runs put("France", "Lyon"), then put("France", "Paris"). What does get("France") return? {
=Paris#Correct. A second put with the same key replaces the old value.
~Lyon#Not quite. The first value is replaced, not kept.
~Lyon and Paris#Not quite. Each key maps to exactly one value.
~null#Not quite. The key is stored, so get finds its latest value.
}

::o1 lookup direction::A Map of String to Integer named ages holds "Sam" mapped to 31. Which call returns 31? {
=ages.get("Sam")#Correct. get takes the key and returns its value.
~ages.get(31)#Not quite. 31 is a value, not a key, so this returns null.
~ages.put("Sam")#Not quite. put stores a key and a value; it does not look anything up.
~ages.get(0)#Not quite. A map has no positions; look values up by key.
}

::o2 missing key::A Map of String to Integer named stock has no key "plum". What does stock.get("plum") return? {
=null#Correct. get returns null when the key is not stored.
~0#Not quite. A map does not invent a default; getOrDefault supplies one.
~It throws an exception#Not quite. get itself does not throw; trouble comes later if null is used as an int.
~-1#Not quite. -1 is a common search convention, but get returns null.
}

::o2 safe test::Which condition is true exactly when the map stock has an entry for "plum"? {
=stock.containsKey("plum")#Correct. containsKey reports whether the key is stored.
~stock.get("plum") \=\= 0#Not quite. When the key is missing, get returns null and unboxing it throws NullPointerException.
~stock.getOrDefault("plum", 0) \=\= 0#Not quite. That is also true when "plum" is stored with the value 0.
~stock.size() \=\= 1#Not quite. The size counts all keys, not whether "plum" is one of them.
}

::o3 count result::A word count adds 1 to each word's count as it reads the words go, stop, go, go. What does the finished map hold? {
=go mapped to 3 and stop mapped to 1#Correct. Each key appears once, with the number of times its word was read.
~go, stop, go, go as four separate entries#Not quite. A map keeps each key once; repeats raise its count.
~go mapped to 1 and stop mapped to 1#Not quite. That records only whether each word appeared, not how often.
~go mapped to 4 and stop mapped to 1#Not quite. go was read three times, not four.
}

::o3 first occurrence::A word count reads each word's current count with plain get and adds 1. What happens the first time a new word is read? {
=It throws NullPointerException, because get returns null and null cannot be unboxed to add 1#Correct. The first occurrence needs containsKey or getOrDefault with 0.
~The count starts at 0 automatically#Not quite. get returns null for a missing key, not 0.
~The word is skipped#Not quite. Nothing skips it; the addition fails.
~The count becomes 1 because null counts as 0#Not quite. Java never treats null as 0; unboxing it throws.
}
